00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Speaker 1: We have a voicemail number.&nbsp;
That number is two one nine two. David P.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.560
Excellent voice mail today about the minimum wage&nbsp;
and its effect on prices. Take a listen to this.&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:17.120
Speaker 6: Hey, David, it's after I was calling to&nbsp;
see if you saw this new article about supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.920
they're raising their average wage to fifteen&nbsp;
dollars an hour. Right. And in order to do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:29.200
they've raised their prices four percent. Mm hmm.&nbsp;
And I thought it was really interesting because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:37.120
I'm curious, is that four percent the apocalypse&nbsp;
that conservatives have been talking about, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.480
wages were raised to living wages&nbsp;
like fifteen dollars an hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:49.280
because I can't fathom choosing a four percent&nbsp;
discount if it meant the person that was serving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.320
me was going to pay significantly less.
Speaker 1: Absolutely. So remember when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:00.720
Obamacare passed, Papa John's went nuts saying if&nbsp;
we have to give our employees health care, we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.520
going to have to add 10 cents to the cost of every&nbsp;
pizza, to which I said I don't go to Papa John's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:11.280
but that sounds great to me. If you came to me&nbsp;
and said, David, would you pay 10 cents more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.040
for your pizza for the knowledge that every&nbsp;
employee has health care? Of course I would.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:21.040
And you know, a chicken burrito at Chipotle is six&nbsp;
dollars and fifty cents. A four percent increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.600
would be 26 cents. So now you're saying if&nbsp;
I pay 676 for my burrito instead of 650,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.720
everybody makes fifteen bucks an hour and&nbsp;
everybody now has more money to go out and spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.480
at other businesses. That sounds fantastic.&nbsp;
There's another example from McDonald's from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.600
a few years ago that the price of a Big Mac would&nbsp;
have to go up by about 10 cents if everybody got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.160
whatever minimum wage raise was being proposed&nbsp;
at the time. That sounds great. And the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.600
why it's so small is that labor is only part of&nbsp;
the total cost of a lot of these places. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.280
have real estate costs, they have insurance, they&nbsp;
have management. They have all this other stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:04.560
the food costs themselves. You can give everybody&nbsp;
a much better wage and you pay 26 extra cents for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.760
a burrito, which even for people who don't make&nbsp;
that much money, is not a dramatic change to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.280
able to say, well, everybody now makes 15 bucks&nbsp;
an hour. So I'm not surprised to hear the numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.800
